Good, now the question is specific. Three points, no more:

1) Encode direction. Routing for text features uses the fields 'encoding' and 'direction' — there is no free-form action verb per feature. For UTF-8 encoding: encoding=utf8, direction=text_to_native_units. The native buffer is a plain decimal integer byte array in 'native_units' — NOT hex, NOT base64 — and it includes the trailing zero byte; 'terminator=0' is reported on its own line. Multi-byte (non-ASCII) characters are preserved as their multi-byte sequences, one decimal integer per byte. Public case, verbatim:
{"description": "Converting text to zero-terminated UTF-8 native byte units preserves ASCII and multi-byte characters.", "cases": [{"input": {"encoding": "utf8", "direction": "text_to_native_units", "text": "Hello World!\n"}, "expected_output": "encoding=utf8\nnative_units=[72,101,108,108,111,32,87,111,114,108,100,33,10,0]\nterminator=0\n"}]}

2) Decode direction. encoding=utf8, direction=native_units_to_text, input key 'bytes' (integer array). Without a 'length' field, decoding stops before the first zero byte — the zero byte is NOT included in text_code_units or the returned text; output lines: encoding=utf8, text_json="...", text_code_units=[...]. With a 'length' field: exactly that many bytes are decoded regardless of zero bytes within them — embedded zero bytes are preserved in the text (that is your embedded-nulls edge case; it only round-trips when length is explicit). Public case, verbatim:
{"description": "Decoding UTF-8 native byte units returns the requested text, using either the first zero terminator or an explicit byte length.", "cases": [{"input": {"encoding": "utf8", "direction": "native_units_to_text", "bytes": [72,101,108,108,111,32,87,111,114,108,100,33,10,0]}, "expected_output": "encoding=utf8\ntext_json=\"Hello World!\\n\"\ntext_code_units=[72,101,108,108,111,32,87,111,114,108,100,33,10]\n"}]}

3) Errors. Malformed UTF-8 byte sequences produce exactly two lines: error=invalid_text_encoding followed by encoding=utf8. That is a language-neutral portable error category, not a Dart exception class name. Public case, verbatim:
{"description": "Invalid UTF-8 inputs and unsupported null or negative-length requests are reported with language-neutral error categories.", "cases": [{"input": {"encoding": "utf8", "direction": "native_units_to_text", "bytes": [128, 0]}, "expected_output": "error=invalid_text_encoding\nencoding=utf8\n"}]}

These case files are the black-box contract. Do not invent extra key names beyond what you see here.
